<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8" />
<title>Cheques</title>
<style>
/* ============================================================================
   US business voucher cheque - cheque in the middle (3.5in / 3.5in / 4in)
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Physical layout only. Every value is pre-formatted by the renderer, so this
   template can be edited from the Template admin surface without touching code.

   Geometry (Letter 215.9mm x 279.4mm):
     voucher stub1 top      0mm .. 88.9mm   (3.5in)
     cheque body   top   88.9mm .. 177.8mm  (8.5in x 3.5in)
     voucher stub2 top  177.8mm .. 279.4mm  (4in)
   MICR clear band = bottom 15.9mm (5/8in) of the cheque body: nothing but the
   magnetic line may be printed there, or the reader rejects the item.

   Everything on the cheque is positioned INSIDE `.cheque`, so the three
   positions of this family differ only in the block marked "band geometry"
   below - move `.cheque` and the whole face moves with it.

   Pre-printed stock: the stock already carries the company/bank identity, the
   cheque number, the "$", "PAY TO THE ORDER OF", "DOLLARS" and the MICR line.
   Those elements carry Model.PrePrintedClass (= "noprint"), so they stay
   VISIBLE on screen (full simulation for proof-reading) and become
   visibility:hidden when printing - hidden but still occupying space.
   ========================================================================= */

@@page { size: auto; margin: 0; }

* { margin: 0; padding: 0; box-sizing: border-box; }

body {
    background: #eef0f3;
    font-family: Arial, "Helvetica Neue", Helvetica, sans-serif;
    color: #000;
    -webkit-print-color-adjust: exact;
    print-color-adjust: exact;
}

.cheque-page {
    position: relative;
    width: 215.9mm;
    height: 279.4mm;
    margin: 0 auto 8mm;
    background: #fff;
    box-shadow: 0 1mm 3mm rgba(0, 0, 0, .18);
    overflow: hidden;
    page-break-after: always;
}
.cheque-page:last-child { page-break-after: auto; }

.abs { position: absolute; }

/* ---------- band geometry (the ONLY difference between top / middle / bottom) ---------- */
.cheque { top: 88.9mm; height: 88.9mm; }
.stub-1 { top: 0mm; height: 88.9mm; }
.stub-2 { top: 177.8mm; height: 101.6mm; }

/* ---------- cheque body ---------- */
.cheque { position: absolute; left: 0; width: 215.9mm; }
.cheque.blank-stock { border: 0.3mm solid #444; }

.issuer { left: 10mm; top: 5mm; width: 96mm; }
.issuer-logo { max-height: 11mm; max-width: 45mm; display: block; margin-bottom: 1mm; }
.issuer-name { font-size: 11pt; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .2mm; }
.issuer-line { font-size: 7pt; line-height: 1.35; color: #222; }

/* Declared heights on the two top-right blocks are load-bearing, not decoration:
   the courtesy-amount clear area below is measured against them (see `.courtesy`). */
.cheque-no { right: 10mm; top: 5mm; width: 60mm; height: 6mm; text-align: right; font-size: 12pt; font-weight: 700; }
.cheque-no-label { font-size: 7pt; font-weight: 400; color: #444; }

.date-block { right: 10mm; top: 16mm; width: 62mm; height: 8mm; text-align: right; }
.date-label { font-size: 6.5pt; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .4mm; color: #444; }
.date-value { font-size: 10pt; letter-spacing: .6mm; border-bottom: 0.25mm solid #000; padding-top: .6mm; }

.pay-label { left: 10mm; top: 33.5mm; font-size: 6.5pt; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .3mm; color: #333; }
.payee { left: 10mm; top: 37mm; width: 132mm; font-size: 11pt; font-weight: 700; border-bottom: 0.25mm solid #000; padding-bottom: .6mm; white-space: nowrap; overflow: hidden; }
.payee-address { left: 10mm; top: 43.5mm; width: 132mm; font-size: 7pt; line-height: 1.3; color: #222; }

/* ---- Courtesy amount ----
   A clear area around the amount field is what lets a reader isolate it. This
   layout is US stock and so is not governed by CPA-006, but its geometry was
   copied from the Canadian one and inherited the same too-tight numbers; the
   6.4mm (0.25in) figure CPA-006 section 5.4.3 states is the only written
   dimension we have, and honouring it here costs 2mm of layout.

   Geometry (page is 215.9mm wide):
     amount field 148.9mm .. 205.9mm   x   33mm .. 42mm   ("$" + box together)
     clear area   142.5mm .. 212.3mm   x   26.6mm .. 48.4mm
     above  .date-block  ends at 24mm    (needs <= 26.6mm)
     below  .legal       starts at 50mm  (needs >= 48.4mm)
     left   .payee       ends at 142mm   (needs <= 142.5mm) */
.courtesy { right: 10mm; top: 33mm; width: 52mm; height: 9mm; }
.courtesy-box { position: absolute; inset: 0; border: 0.3mm solid #000; }
.courtesy-sign { position: absolute; left: -5mm; top: 1.4mm; font-size: 11pt; font-weight: 700; }
.courtesy-value { position: absolute; right: 1.6mm; top: 1.6mm; font-size: 12pt; font-weight: 700; white-space: nowrap; }

.legal { left: 10mm; top: 50mm; width: 166mm; font-size: 9.5pt; border-bottom: 0.25mm solid #000; padding-bottom: .6mm; white-space: nowrap; overflow: hidden; }
.legal-currency { left: 178mm; top: 50mm; font-size: 8pt; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .2mm; }

.bank { left: 10mm; top: 57mm; width: 92mm; font-size: 7.5pt; line-height: 1.35; color: #222; }
.bank-name { font-weight: 700; font-size: 8.5pt; }

.memo-label { left: 10mm; top: 68mm; font-size: 6.5pt; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .3mm; color: #333; }
.memo { left: 25mm; top: 67.5mm; width: 76mm; font-size: 7.5pt; border-bottom: 0.25mm solid #000; padding-bottom: .4mm; white-space: nowrap; overflow: hidden; }

.signature { left: 128mm; top: 56mm; width: 78mm; }
.signature-image { height: 10mm; display: flex; align-items: flex-end; justify-content: center; }
.signature-image img { max-height: 10mm; max-width: 70mm; }
.signature-line { border-top: 0.25mm solid #000; margin-top: .5mm; }
.signature-name { font-size: 7.5pt; text-align: center; padding-top: .4mm; }
.signature-title { font-size: 6.5pt; text-align: center; color: #333; }

.micr-band { left: 0; bottom: 0; width: 215.9mm; height: 15.9mm; }
.micr-line { position: absolute; left: 32mm; bottom: 5mm; font-family: "MICR E13B", "E-13B", "GnuMICR", monospace; font-size: 12pt; letter-spacing: .5mm; white-space: nowrap; }
.micr-readable { position: absolute; left: 32mm; bottom: 1.2mm; font-size: 5.5pt; letter-spacing: .4mm; color: #555; white-space: nowrap; }

.preview-watermark {
    left: 0; top: 22mm; width: 215.9mm;
    text-align: center;
    font-size: 30pt;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 2mm;
    color: rgba(190, 30, 45, .22);
    transform: rotate(-12deg);
    pointer-events: none;
}

/* ---------- voucher stubs ---------- */
.stub { position: absolute; left: 0; width: 215.9mm; padding: 8mm 10mm; border-top: 0.2mm dashed #999; }
.stub-head { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: baseline; margin-bottom: 4mm; }
.stub-issuer { font-size: 10pt; font-weight: 700; }
.stub-copy { font-size: 7pt; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .4mm; color: #555; }
.stub-table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 8pt; }
.stub-table th { width: 34mm; text-align: left; font-weight: 400; color: #555; padding: 1.2mm 0; vertical-align: top; }
.stub-table td { padding: 1.2mm 0; vertical-align: top; }
.stub-amount { font-weight: 700; }

/* Stub lines supplied by the application (file no., matter, work order, ...).
   A hairline separates them from the fixed rows above; `+` sibling selector so
   only the FIRST extra row carries it. Nothing matches these rules when the
   application supplies no lines, which is why the stub is unchanged without them. */
.stub-extra th, .stub-extra td { border-top: 0.2mm solid #e6e8ec; padding-top: 2mm; }
.stub-extra + .stub-extra th, .stub-extra + .stub-extra td { border-top: 0; padding-top: 1.2mm; }

@@media print {
    body { background: #fff; }
    .cheque-page { margin: 0; box-shadow: none; }
    /* Pre-printed stock: keep the space, drop the ink. */
    .noprint { visibility: hidden; }
}

/* ---------- specimen mode (layout picker) ----------
   A specimen is rendered from placeholder data so somebody choosing a layout
   can hold it against the paper in the printer. The question it has to answer
   is "what does this layout put on MY stock", and on pre-printed stock the
   answer is "the elements below are already there - the printer only adds the
   variable data".

   `.noprint` alone cannot say that: it lives inside @@media print, so on screen
   those elements render exactly as they do on blank stock, and the two stock
   types look the same in a thumbnail. Fading them is the signal that survives
   on screen and at thumbnail scale. One knob - change the opacity, or swap it
   for a grayscale/dashed treatment, whatever reads best on your stock.

   Scoped under `.specimen`, which only a specimen puts on the page root: a real
   cheque and a payment preview match nothing here, so their output is
   untouched (a preview SHOULD show the full face - you are proof-reading a
   cheque then, not comparing paper). */
.specimen .noprint { opacity: .28; }
</style>
</head>
<body>
@foreach (var chk in Model.Checks)
{
    @* The ternary supplies its own leading space, so a real cheque still renders
       exactly `class="cheque-page"` - byte-identical to before specimen mode. *@
    <section class="cheque-page@(Model.IsSpecimen ? " specimen" : "")" style="@Model.OffsetStyle">

        @* ============================ cheque body ============================ *@
        <div class="cheque @(Model.IsPrePrinted ? "" : "blank-stock")">

            @if (Model.IsPreview)
            {
                <div class="abs preview-watermark">@Model.PreviewLabel</div>
            }

            @if (Model.Issuer.HasIdentity)
            {
                <div class="abs issuer @Model.PrePrintedClass">
                    @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Model.Issuer.LogoUrl))
                    {
                        <img class="issuer-logo" src="@Model.Issuer.LogoUrl" alt="" />
                    }
                    <div class="issuer-name">@Model.Issuer.Name</div>
                    @foreach (var line in Model.Issuer.AddressLines)
                    {
                        <div class="issuer-line">@line</div>
                    }
                    @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Model.Issuer.Phone))
                    {
                        <div class="issuer-line">@Model.Issuer.Phone</div>
                    }
                </div>
            }

            <div class="abs cheque-no @Model.PrePrintedClass">
                <span class="cheque-no-label">No.</span> @chk.CheckNumberText
            </div>

            @* ISO date: unambiguous however it is read, and the framework must not
               guess a national date order. Change it here if your stock carries a
               pre-printed MM/DD/YYYY guide. *@
            <div class="abs date-block">
                <div class="date-label @Model.PrePrintedClass">Date</div>
                <div class="date-value">@chk.IssueDateIso</div>
            </div>

            <div class="abs pay-label @Model.PrePrintedClass">Pay to the order of</div>
            <div class="abs payee">@chk.PayeeName</div>
            @if (chk.PayeeAddressLines.Count > 0)
            {
                <div class="abs payee-address">
                    @foreach (var line in chk.PayeeAddressLines)
                    {
                        <div>@line</div>
                    }
                </div>
            }

            <div class="abs courtesy">
                <div class="courtesy-box @Model.PrePrintedClass"></div>
                <div class="courtesy-sign @Model.PrePrintedClass">$</div>
                <div class="courtesy-value">@chk.AmountText</div>
            </div>

            <div class="abs legal">@chk.AmountInWordsText</div>
            <div class="abs legal-currency @Model.PrePrintedClass">@chk.CurrencyLabel</div>

            <div class="abs bank @Model.PrePrintedClass">
                <div class="bank-name">@Model.Bank.Name</div>
                @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Model.Bank.RoutingLine))
                {
                    <div>@Model.Bank.RoutingLine</div>
                }
                @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Model.Bank.AccountName))
                {
                    <div>@Model.Bank.AccountName</div>
                }
            </div>

            <div class="abs memo-label @Model.PrePrintedClass">Memo</div>
            <div class="abs memo">@chk.Memo</div>

            <div class="abs signature">
                <div class="signature-image">
                    @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Model.Issuer.SignatureImageUrl))
                    {
                        <img src="@Model.Issuer.SignatureImageUrl" alt="" />
                    }
                </div>
                <div class="signature-line @Model.PrePrintedClass"></div>
                <div class="signature-name">@Model.Issuer.SignatureName</div>
                <div class="signature-title">@Model.Issuer.SignatureTitle</div>
            </div>

            <div class="abs micr-band">
                @if (Model.ShowMicr)
                {
                    <div class="micr-line">@chk.MicrGlyphs</div>
                    <div class="micr-readable">@chk.MicrLine</div>
                }
            </div>
        </div>

        @* ============================ voucher stubs ============================ *@
        <div class="stub stub-1">
            <div class="stub-head">
                <span class="stub-issuer">@Model.Issuer.Name</span>
                <span class="stub-copy">Voucher copy 1 - payee</span>
            </div>
            <table class="stub-table">
                <tr><th>Check number</th><td>@chk.CheckNumberText</td></tr>
                <tr><th>Date</th><td>@chk.IssueDateIso</td></tr>
                <tr><th>Payee</th><td>@chk.PayeeName</td></tr>
                <tr><th>Amount</th><td class="stub-amount">@chk.AmountText @chk.Currency</td></tr>
                @* The four rows above are unconditional: a cheque always has a number, a date, a
                   payee and an amount, and a blank one of those means something went wrong and
                   should be visible. The three below are only sometimes carried by the payment.

                   A printed label with nothing after it reads as MISSING information rather than
                   absent information - whoever files the stub cannot tell an empty memo from a
                   memo that failed to print. It also spends stub height, and height is the one
                   thing this surface has none of.

                   ! The supplied lines further down follow the OPPOSITE rule on purpose: there a
                   label with no value is a section heading, so CheckStubLineLimits.Normalize keeps
                   it and drops only lines with no LABEL. Do not "make them consistent". *@
                @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(chk.PaymentNumber))
                {
                    <tr><th>Payment</th><td>@chk.PaymentNumber</td></tr>
                }
                @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(chk.Reference))
                {
                    <tr><th>Reference</th><td>@chk.Reference</td></tr>
                }
                @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(chk.Memo))
                {
                    <tr><th>Memo</th><td>@chk.Memo</td></tr>
                }
                @* Application-supplied stub lines, appended AFTER the fixed rows so
                   a stub without them looks exactly as it always has. *@
                @foreach (var line in chk.StubLines)
                {
                    <tr class="stub-extra"><th>@line.Label</th><td>@line.Value</td></tr>
                }
            </table>
        </div>

        <div class="stub stub-2">
            <div class="stub-head">
                <span class="stub-issuer">@Model.Issuer.Name</span>
                <span class="stub-copy">Voucher copy 2 - file</span>
            </div>
            <table class="stub-table">
                <tr><th>Check number</th><td>@chk.CheckNumberText</td></tr>
                <tr><th>Date</th><td>@chk.IssueDateIso</td></tr>
                <tr><th>Payee</th><td>@chk.PayeeName</td></tr>
                <tr><th>Amount</th><td class="stub-amount">@chk.AmountText @chk.Currency</td></tr>
                @* The four rows above are unconditional: a cheque always has a number, a date, a
                   payee and an amount, and a blank one of those means something went wrong and
                   should be visible. The three below are only sometimes carried by the payment.

                   A printed label with nothing after it reads as MISSING information rather than
                   absent information - whoever files the stub cannot tell an empty memo from a
                   memo that failed to print. It also spends stub height, and height is the one
                   thing this surface has none of.

                   ! The supplied lines further down follow the OPPOSITE rule on purpose: there a
                   label with no value is a section heading, so CheckStubLineLimits.Normalize keeps
                   it and drops only lines with no LABEL. Do not "make them consistent". *@
                @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(chk.PaymentNumber))
                {
                    <tr><th>Payment</th><td>@chk.PaymentNumber</td></tr>
                }
                @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(chk.Reference))
                {
                    <tr><th>Reference</th><td>@chk.Reference</td></tr>
                }
                @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(chk.Memo))
                {
                    <tr><th>Memo</th><td>@chk.Memo</td></tr>
                }
                @* Application-supplied stub lines, appended AFTER the fixed rows so
                   a stub without them looks exactly as it always has. *@
                @foreach (var line in chk.StubLines)
                {
                    <tr class="stub-extra"><th>@line.Label</th><td>@line.Value</td></tr>
                }
            </table>
        </div>

    </section>
}
</body>
</html>
